TI  - CONNECTION BETWEEN GSH (GLUTATHIONE) BLOOD-LEVELS AND MEAT AND FAT GROWTH AND VITALITY OF DIFFERENT SHEEP BREEDS AND CROSSES
T2  - JOURNAL OF ANIMAL BREEDING AND GENETICS
AB  - Interrelationships between GSH contents and growth capacity, possible dependence of mortality on GSH metabolism and differences in the GSH concentration were investigated. Samples were taken from 148 reared and 12 perished lambs of Merionoland, Eastfriesian milk and Black faced mutton sheep and their crossbreds. The lambs were raised with their mothers, weighed fortnightly. A random sample of 90 lambs were slaughtered at a weight of 42-45 kg and carcass dissected. At the age of 7,21, 77 and 125 days whole blood GSH was measured. Following results were obtained: 1. The GSH content is significantly positively correlated with body weight as well as with growth intensity, above all to the daily meat gain both between and within groups, which indicates the importance of GSH for protein synthesis and growth. 2. In comparison to reared animals, perished lambs show a significantly reduced GSH level, pointing to an insufficient adaptation of GSH metabolism and therefore presumably to an insufficient protection against oxidative damage. 3. Though there are to some extent statistically significant differences between the groups, heterotic effects and maternal and paternal effects are not uniform in all crosses.
