TI  - EFFECT OF DIFFERENTIAL PARITY THROUGH 5 GENERATIONS ON INCIDENCE OF TUMORS IN C3HF MICE
T2  - JNCI-JOURNAL OF THE NATIONAL CANCER INSTITUTE
AB  - Age-specific rates and incidence of mammary tumors were compared in a primiparous and a multiparous line of inbred mice bred for 5 generations from the same original brother X sister pair of mice free of the milk factor. The primiparous line developed tumors earlier and at a higher rate. First daughters of the multiparous line tended to revert to the higher rate. Virus particles of both types A and B were found. They were morphologically identical with those previously described in mammary tumors. Tumors in the families of both the multiparous and the primiparous lines were randomly distributed. Within the multiparous line, mothers without tumors at death yielded offspring with mammary tumors. A viral agent is transmitted at the time of conception through the cytoplasm of the ova or sperm or both, a concept similar to that of the CO2 sensitivity factor in Drosophila, i.e., hereditary symbiosis.
