AU  - WOOLF, CM
TI  - GENETIC DIVERGENCE AMONG TUMOROUS-HEAD STRAINS OF DROSOPHILA MELANOGASTER
T2  - GENETICS
AB  - Tumor-ous-head strains are maintained in various laboratories in the USA: Arizona State University [tu-h (ASU)], California Institute of Technology [tu-h(CT)], Johns Hopkins University [tu-h(JH)], The Institute for Cancer Research [tu-h (Phil)] and Utah State University [tu-h (60) and tu-h (90)]. All are descended from the strain kept at the University at Utah before 1949. Two different types of 3rd chromosomes (3A and 3B) are segregating in all these strains except tu-h (Phil), which has lost the 3B chromosome containing the Payne inversion and the recessive gene for scarlet eyes, apparently by random chromosome segregation. The 3B chromosome is homozygous lethal. When both the 3A and 3B chromosomes are present in a strain, female heterokaryotypes (3A/3B) produce more offspring than female homokaryotypes (3A/3A). Furthermore, heterokaryotypes of both sexes are more viable than homokaryotypes. The reduced productivity of the female homokaryotypes is under poly-genic control. Genetic divergence has occurred among the various strains for this fitness character. In some of the strains [tu-h(CT) and tu-h (90)], the female homokaryotypes show low productivity, in another [tu-h (ASU)] the productivity is moderate, while in others [tu-h (60) and tu-h(JH)] it is relatively high[long dash]A 3B chromosome from the tu-h (ASU) strain was inserted into the tu-h (Phil) strain, creating a new strain symbolized tu-h (Phil-3B). Female heterokaryotypes from the tu-h(Phil-3B) strain are more productive than female homokaryotypes. Furthermore, the fitness of the tu-h (Phil-3B) strain is increased over that of the tu-h (Phil) strain, using as a criterion of fitness the mean number of offspring produced each day or the total number of offspring produced in half-pint milk bottles.[long dash]Genetic divergence has also occurred among the strains for a polygenic system affecting the frequency of aspermia. Three of the strains [tu-h (ASU), tu-h (60) and tu-h (90)] show a relatively high frequency of aspermia (17%). The frequency is low in the other strains (<2.5%).[long dash]These results serve as evidence for the genetic divergence that can occur among laboratory stocks of D. melanogaster for polygenic systems, even those affecting important fitness characters.
