AU  - Momtaz, YA
TI  - Listeria monocytogenesis and abortion: A case study of pregnant women in Iran
T2  - AFRICAN JOURNAL OF MICROBIOLOGY RESEARCH
AB  - Listeria monocytogenes is the causative agent of serious infection such as meningoencephalitis and septicemia with about 25% mortality rate. The frequency is as high as 50% in neonates. Although Listeriosis is known increasingly as a serious threat for health, there is little study in some countries, especially Asian countries. This study was conducted to determine the effect of L. monocytogenes on pregnant Iranian women. Isolation of this gram positive Bacillus as the cause of most abortions in pregnant women by bacteriologic method is not so convincing. However, antilisteria antibodies can be detected by indirect fluorescent antibody (IFA) method. The relationship between Listeriosis and abortion was investigated for a year in Jahrom City of Fars Province in Iran. The clinical history and patient charts were reviewed, and IFA was done on patients' sera. 204 women were selected randomly and divided into six age groups: 16 - 20, 21 - 25, 26 - 30, 31 - 35, 36 - 40 and 41 - 45. Sera from both patients and control group were used for investigating Listeria specific antibody by indirect immunofluorescent method. The result showed that Listeria has been a causative agent of 12.5% of abortions (meaningful statistically). The results of serologic study showed that 25 cases of test group and 7 cases of control group had an antibody titer of > 1/160 against this microorganism. In the test group of 25 patients who had antibody titer > 1/160, only 12 patients had 1/320 titer, while of 7 patients in the control group, 3 patients had 1/320 titer. Therefore, in the test group, 24.5% had a titer of > 1/160 and 11.8% had a titer of 1/320. In the control group, the rates of the above antibody titers were 6.9 and 2.9%, respectively. Abortion was found to significantly occur most in age group of 36 - 40 years and the least in 21 - 25 years old. No incidence of abortion caused by L. monocytogenes was seen in the age group of 16 - 20 years and 25 - 30 years. Other related factors as special drug consumption, infections other than listeriosis during pregnancy, using non pasteurized dairy products, contact with domestic animal and multiplicity of abortion during pregnancy have been evaluated.
