TI  - QUANTITATIVE FEED RESTRICTION OF PEKIN BREEDER DUCKS DURING THE REARING PERIOD AND ITS EFFECT ON SUBSEQUENT PRODUCTIVITY
T2  - SOUTH AFRICAN JOURNAL OF ANIMAL SCIENCE
AB  - Six male and 24 female Pekin ducks/pen were randomly allocated to 12 pens to test the effects of quantitative feed restriction during the rearing period on subsequent reproductive performance. The feed treatments during the rearing periods were ad lib. (80%, 60% and 40%) with 3 replicates of each treatment. During the laying period a duck breeding diet was fed ad lib. to all groups. As the degree of quantitative feed restriction during the rearing period increased, changes were noted. Body mass at 22 wk of age decreased, these differences becoming negligible at 62 wk of age. Feed intake to 22 wk of age was lowered, the reduction in intake being proportional to the degree of restriction. The number of days to sexual maturity was delayed, resulting in an increase in average egg mass. The percentage fertility and hatchability of eggs sets increased. The mortality during the laying period decreased. Significant differences were found between treatments with regard to carcass moisture and carcass fat. There were also significant differences between treatments in respect of the adjusted and unadjusted viscera masses giving some idea of the physiological phenomena that accompanied feed restriction. The economic advantage of restriction increased.
