TI  - The Volume of Nipple Aspirate Fluid Is Not Affected by 6 Months of Treatment with Soy Foods in Premenopausal Women
T2  - JOURNAL OF NUTRITION
AB  - Rased on the hypothesis that soy food consumption may influence breast tissue act vity, we examined its effect on the production of nipple aspirate fluid (NAF), a poss ble ndicator of breast cancer risk. Of 310 premenopausal women screened, 112 (36%) produced at least 10 mu of NAF, tie minimum for study participat on In a crossover des gn, we randomized 96 women to 2 groups who, in reverse order, consumed a high-soy diet with 2 soy servings/d (1 serving = 177 nil. soy milk, 260 tofu, or 230 soy nuts) and a low-soy diet with <3 servings/wk of soy for 6 mo each separated by a 1-mo washout: period During each diet period, 3 NAF samples were obtained (baseline and 3 and 6 mo) using a FirstCyte Asp rator and 4 urine samples (baseline and 1, 3, and 6 mo) were analyzed for isoflavono ds by liquid chromatography tandem MS. Adherence to the study protocol according to 24-h dietary recalls and urinary isoflavonoid excretion was high. The drop-out rate was 15% in = 141; 82 women completed the intervention. The 2 groups produced similar mean NAP volumes at baseline (P = 0.95) but differed in age ana previous soy intake and in their response to the intervention (P = 0.03). In both groups, NAF volume decreased during the first 3 mo of the high-soy diet period and returned to baseline at 6 mo, but there was no effect of the high-soy diet on NAF volume (P = 0.50 for deit; P-interaction = 0.21 or diet with time). Contrary to an earlier report, soy foods in amounts consumed by Asians did not increase breast tissue activity as assessed by NAF volume. J. Nutr. 141: 626-630, 2011.
