TI  - URINARY CYCLIC-AMP ANALYZED AS A FUNCTION OF SERUM-CALCIUM AND PARATHYROID-HORMONE IN DIFFERENTIAL-DIAGNOSIS OF HYPERCALCEMIA
T2  - JOURNAL OF CLINICAL INVESTIGATION
AB  - Urinary cyclic AMP (UcAMP) appropriate for the serum Ca concentration was determined in normal subjects during the base-line state and during alteration in their serum Ca concentrations by saline and Ca infusions. This was compared to the UcAMP in 76 patients with hypercalcemia and 5 patients with hypocalcemia. In 54 of 56 patients with primary hyperparathyroidism, the UcAMP was inappropriately high for their serum Ca concentration, the 2 exceptions having renal failure. In 4 patients with vitamin D intoxication, sarcoidosis, milk-alkali syndrome and thiazide-induced hypercalcemia and in 5 patients with hypocalcemia due to hypoparathyroidism, the UcAMP was appropriately low for their serum Ca concentration. In 16 patients with nonparathyroid neoplasms, 10 had UcAMP levels that were inappropriately high suggesting ectopic parathyroid hormone (PTH)-mediated hypercalcemia and 6 had UcAMP levels that were appropriately low, suggesting that their hypercalcemia was due to osteolytic factors other than PTH. Correlations between UcAMP, serum Ca concentration and carboxy-terminal immunoreactive PTH suggest that random UcAMP is a sensitive accurate reflection of circulating biologically active PTH. If there is adequate renal function (serum creatinine concentration less than 2.0 mg/dl), a random UcAMP expressed as .mu.mol/g creatinine and analyzed as a function of the serum Ca concentration completely separates patients with PTH and non-PTH-mediated hypercalcemia.
