TI  - EFFECTS OF AGE ON THE CHANGES IN LEVELS OF UREA, TOTAL PROTEIN, NONPROTEIN NITROGEN, AMINO NITROGEN AND URIC-ACID IN BLOOD-SERUM OF CALVES
T2  - ZIVOCISNA VYROBA
AB  - Lack of nutrients is manifested the most in young animals, in particular in first days of life and later at transition from dairy nutrition to plant one. In these "critical periods" different metabolic and consequently also health disorders of calves may be apparent.
   The study was aimed at examining some indicators of nitrogen metabolism of calves from calving to the age of 6 months along with evaluation of its dynamics.
   Two subsequent trials included 21 or 24, respectively, randomly selected calves of the Black Pied breed from calving to 175 days of age. Blood samples were collected on regular basis, three hours after morning feeding through punction from v. jugularis.
   Variational-statistical evaluation of levels of studied parameters are presented in Tab. I, testing of differences are in Tab. II and testing of age dynamics is on Figs. 1 to 5.
   A strong correlation dependence (I(yx)=0.948 or I(yx)=0.999, respectively) was found between urea content and age. Its levels were of rising tendency with age, this being confirmed also by graphic expression of their pattern.
   There was a strong correlation dependence between total protein content and age (I(yx)=0.858 or I(xy)=0.721). The CB level was of slightly rising tendency in the first trial with more marked rise at the age of 146 days, this was less or more on more balanced level in the second trial with more significant decline at the age of 14 to 35 days with increase at the age of 119 and 147 days.
   There was a strong correlation dependence between non-protein nitrogen (NN) and age (I(yx)=0.991 or I(yx)=0.702). The most marked changes in NN content were observed usually in the course of first 21 days when at the age of 5 days a sharp increase and subsequent marked decline in values. Then its level was kept on less or more balanced horizontal line with increase at the age of 119 days in the first trial and 91 days in the second trial.
   There was a strong correlation dependence (I(yx)=0.899 or I(yx)=0.756) between amino nitrogen content (AN) and age. The AN dynamics was of slightly increasing tendency with slightly lower age of 175 days. More significant decline of AN values was found in the second trial at the age from 7 to 21 days. There was a strong correlation dependence (I(yx)=0.997 or I(yx)=0.998) between uric acid content (KM) and age. KM level was of rising tendency with age in both trials. Reciprocal dependences among indicators under review (Fig. 6) were calculated as an illustration of reciprocal correlation relationships. Medium strong to strong reciprocal dependences were observed among most indicators under study.
   It can be said on the basis of our results that: 1) The most significant changes occurred on the levels of studied indicators usually in the course of first 35 days. These changes can be explained by sharp development of immunity system of calves, higher sensitivity to diseases and by marked changes in the colostrum composition in the first days. 2) There is a strong correlation dependence (I(yx) > 0.7) between studied indicators and age. 3) Interaction between studied indicators among each other is not of less importance, confirmed by medium strong to strong correlation coefficients with the only exception - between AN and NN in the second trial. 4) Further effect participating mainly in the increased concentration of urea and reduced CB consists in higher occurrence of scours. Urea and CB levels should be recommended for practice for predictions of disease and mortality of calves.
