TI  - Polyphenon E®: a new treatment for external anogenital warts
T2  - BRITISH JOURNAL OF DERMATOLOGY
AB  - P>Background
   External genital warts (EGWs, condylomata acuminata) are a common, highly contagious disease caused by human papillomavirus (HPV), predominantly HPV 6 and HPV 11. Green tea catechins have been identified for their immunostimulatory, antiproliferative and antitumour properties. Two phase III trials evaluated treatment of EGWs with ointment containing a mixture of green tea catechins (Polyphenon E (R), U.S. adopted name: sinecatechins).
   Objectives
   To obtain additional data on the efficacy and safety of Polyphenon E (R) ointment in the treatment of EGWs from two randomized, double-blind, vehicle-controlled trials.
   Methods
   Men and women aged >= 18 years (n = 1005), with two to 30 EGWs (12-600 mm2 total area) applied vehicle (G(Veh); n = 207), Polyphenon E (R) ointment 10% (G(10%); n = 401) or Polyphenon E (R) ointment 15% (G(15%); n = 397) three times daily until complete clearance of all EGWs (baseline + new EGWs) or for a maximum of 16 weeks.
   Results
   A total of 1004 patients were evaluable for safety and 986 for efficacy; 838 completed treatment after 16 weeks. Complete clearance of all EGWs was obtained in 53 center dot 6% (G(10%)) and 54 center dot 9% (G(15%)) of patients with Polyphenon E (R) vs. vehicle (35 center dot 4%) (P < 0 center dot 001). Statistically significant differences in clearance rates appeared after 6 weeks of active treatment. Odds ratios vs. G(Veh) for G(10%) [2 center dot 10; 95% confidence interval (CI) 1 center dot 49-2 center dot 98] and G(15%) (2 center dot 22; 95% CI 1 center dot 57-3 center dot 14) indicated about a twofold higher chance of complete clearance under active treatment. Time to complete clearance was shorter with active treatment (hazard ratios 1 center dot 57 and 1 center dot 87, respectively, for G(10%) and G(15%) vs. G(Veh) groups; P < 0 center dot 001). Recurrence rates during follow-up were low and similar across groups: 5 center dot 8%, 6 center dot 8% and 6 center dot 5% (G(Veh), G(10%) and G(15%) groups, respectively). Adverse events were evenly distributed across groups (similar to 30% of patients). Severe local signs were more frequent but moderate in the active treatment groups (1 center dot 5%, 9 center dot 2% and 13 center dot 5% for G(Veh), G(10%) and G(15%) groups, respectively).
   Conclusions
   Polyphenon E (R) ointment is effective and well tolerated in the treatment of EGWs.
