AU  - Szilagyi, A
TI  - Relationships between western non communicable diseases and geographic pattern modifiers based on latitude and lactase distributions
T2  - MEDICAL HYPOTHESES
AB  - Toward the end of the 20th century, rates of multiple "western" industrialization related diseases were reported to diminish with decreasing latitudes. In 1986 it was also reported that inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis) were inversely correlated with national frequencies of population adult lactase non persistence. Several other "western" diseases were also found to be similarly related. The explanation for these non-random distributions was unclear. In the case of latitude, the associated effect of sunshine with vitamin D synthesis and the pleiotropic functions of this vitamin in immunological and antineoplastic functions is a dominant hypothesis. However, failure to consistently link vitamin D with clinical disease courses have left a controversy. In the case of lactase, a close correlation exists between latitude and lactose digestion distributions. This is evident mainly in Europe and to a lesser extent elsewhere. As, such "western" diseases correlate similarly with both ecological markers. It is hypothesized that rather than single factors are responsible for disease distributions it is rather an amalgamation of multiple interactive ecological and environmental variables contribute to the geo-epidemiological distributions of such non communicable diseases. These various factors plausably relate diseases with either or both ecological markers include existence of different temperatures at different latitudes, the effect of latitude per se and diets which modulate the intestinal environment. Additionally the ongoing epidemic of Helicobacter pylori infection may contribute via immunologic manipulations. The recognized relationships between industrialization and western lifestyle diseases can also be traced via some economic metrics to geographic distributions. Still, analyses of latitude and lactase modifiers suggest mutually independent impacts on some diseases. This hypothesis driven discussion examines possible reasons which may account for observations as to why the two ecological markers might define certain non communicable global disease patterns.
